        <p class="profile-detail-region">Life · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Suleiman the Magnificent</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1494 CE – 1566 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">Probably 6 November 1494, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a> on the southern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>; son of Şehzade Selim (later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a>) of the Ottoman dynasty and Hafsa Sultan</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">6 September 1566, in his tent at the siege of Szigetvár in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, one day before the fortress fell; his death was kept secret for 48 days</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a>, then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a>, from the age of seventeen; tenth Ottoman sultan, 1520–1566</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The longest reign of any Ottoman sultan; the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>; the victory at Mohács; the failed siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>; the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>; a single code of sultanic law; the Süleymaniye complex built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a>; poetry under the name Muhibbi</span></div>
        </div>
