    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Suleiman reigned for 46 years, the longest of any Ottoman sultan, and raised the number of the empire's subjects to at least 25 million.</li><li>His armies took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, but were turned back at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1529 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> in 1565.</li><li>He issued a single code of sultanic law that lasted more than three hundred years, and had his eldest surviving son Mustafa and his son Bayezid put to death in the struggle over the succession.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Suleiman wrote poetry in Persian and Turkish under the pen name Muhibbi, &quot;Lover&quot;, and some of his verses have become Turkish proverbs. When his son Mehmed died in 1543 he composed a chronogram whose letters add up to 955, the year in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

