    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1520</span><span class="rf-text">Suleiman succeeds his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> as the tenth Ottoman sultan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1521</span><span class="rf-text">He besieges and takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1522</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> capitulates after a five-month siege, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> are allowed to depart</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1526</span><span class="rf-text">He defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-hungary">Louis II of Hungary</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a>, where the king and much of the Hungarian aristocracy are killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1529</span><span class="rf-text">His siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> fails, the first defeat inflicted on him</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1535</span><span class="rf-text">He makes a grand entrance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> during the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1553</span><span class="rf-text">His eldest surviving son, Şehzade Mustafa, is killed in his father's tent on his orders</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1566</span><span class="rf-text">He dies in his tent at the siege of Szigetvár and is succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/selim-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/selim-i.jpg" alt="Selim I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nakka%C5%9F_Selim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Naḳḳāş ʿOs̠mān, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Suleiman was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a>, on the southern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, probably on 6 November 1494, although the date is not known with certainty. His father was Şehzade Selim, the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a>; his mother, Hafsa Sultan, was a concubine and convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> of unknown origins, who died in 1534. At the age of seven he began to study science, history, literature, theology and military tactics in the schools of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><p>At seventeen he was appointed governor, first of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a> (Theodosia) and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a>, with a brief tenure at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>. At Manisa the concubine Mahidevran entered his harem, and his first sons were born there, among them Mustafa in about 1516 or 1517. As a young man he befriended Ibrahim, originally a Christian from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parga">Parga</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, who had been captured in a raid during the Ottoman–Venetian War of 1499–1503 and was given to Suleiman as a slave, most likely in 1514. Ibrahim converted to Islam, and Suleiman made him royal falconer and then first officer of the Royal Bedchamber.</p><p>The empire he was to inherit had been built by nine sultans before him. His great-grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> had failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> because of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a>'s defence, and after the defeats of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosniaks">Bosniaks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, the Hungarians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> remained the only formidable force that could block further Ottoman gains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. His father, Selim I, who reigned from 1512 to 1520 and wrote poetry in Persian, had made war with the Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> a high priority.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs.jpg" alt="Battle of Mohács" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_moh%C3%A1csi_csata_(Des_T%C3%BCrckischen_Keysers_Solymon_schlacht_-_So_mit_dem_K%C3%BCnig_von_Hungern_gethan,_Bamberg,_1526).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a>, Suleiman entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and ascended the throne as the tenth Ottoman sultan, on 30 September 1520. A few weeks later the Venetian envoy Bartolomeo Contarini described him as "tall and slender but tough, with a thin and bony face", and reported the rumour that he "enjoys reading, is knowledgeable and shows good judgment". In 1521 the Ottoman-appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> led a revolt.</p><p>Suleiman turned first against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>. He encircled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> on 28 August 1521, with 250,000 soldiers and over 100 ships, and bombarded it from an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. The city became the seat of an Ottoman province. The road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> lay open, but he turned instead to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>. After a five-month siege in 1522 the island capitulated, and he allowed the Knights to depart; the conquest cost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> 50,000 to 60,000 dead from battle and sickness, and Christian claims went higher.</p><p>In 1523 he raised Ibrahim Pasha to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> and commander-in-chief of all the armies, and made him beylerbey of Rumelia, with authority over all Ottoman territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Ibrahim was about thirty and lacked military expertise, and it is said that tongues wagged at a promotion straight from palace service to the two highest offices of the empire. On 29 August 1526 Suleiman defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-hungary">Louis II of Hungary</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a>. The king and much of the Hungarian aristocracy were killed, the royal army was destroyed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a> ended in Hungary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After Mohács <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was partitioned between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy and the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. In the east Suleiman fought three campaigns against the Safavid shah Tahmasp. He made a grand entrance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1535, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-amasya">Peace of Amasya</a> of 1555 gave the Ottomans most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, with access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, and divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> between the two empires. At sea he appointed Khair ad Din, known to Europeans as Barbarossa, admiral-in-chief and charged him with rebuilding the fleet. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> was captured in 1538 and Tripoli in 1551, and he accepted an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, which was renewed in 1542.</p><p>In Turkish he is known as Kanuni, "the Lawgiver". The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> was outside the sultan's power to change, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanun">Kanuns</a>, covering criminal law, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a> and taxation, depended on his will alone. He collected the judgments of the nine sultans before him, removed duplications and contradictions, and, supported by his Grand Mufti <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebussuud-efendi">Ebussuud</a>, issued a single code that was to last more than three hundred years. New criminal legislation prescribed fines for specific offences and reduced the instances requiring death or mutilation. In late 1553 or 1554 he issued a firman denouncing blood libels against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>.</p><p>Under his patronage, imperial societies of craftsmen worked at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a>; a register of 1526 lists 40 societies with over 600 members. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a>, chief court architect from 1538, built for him the Süleymaniye complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> between 1550 and 1557.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Charles_V,_Holy_Roman_Emperor,_seated_(1500%E2%80%931558),_formerly_attributed_to_Titian_(Alte_Pinakothek,_Munich).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>His principal enemies were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and Ferdinand I, the Safavid shah, the Portuguese and the Knights of St. John. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in the autumn of 1529 a reinforced garrison of 16,000 men inflicted the first defeat on him; a second attempt in 1532 was delayed by the siege of Güns and never reached the city. In both cases bad weather forced the army to leave siege equipment behind, and supply lines were overstretched. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Shah Tahmasp</a> avoided pitched battle, gave up territory and used scorched earth tactics, so that the campaign of 1548–1549 was abandoned and that of 1553 ended in stalemate. In 1535 Charles V led a Holy League of 26,700 soldiers to victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, and an Ottoman fleet failed against the Portuguese at Diu in 1538.</p><p>There was opposition within the empire as well. While he campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1526, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> revolted under Kalender Çelebi. At court the rapid rise and vast wealth of Ibrahim Pasha made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> many enemies; Ibrahim had the finance secretary İskender Çelebi sentenced to death, and by supporting Mustafa as heir he came into dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrem-sultan">Hürrem Sultan</a>. After thirteen years in office he fell from favour, and Suleiman had him strangled in his sleep.</p><p>Hürrem, a former slave from Ruthenia, became the sultan's legal wife in 1534, to the astonishment of observers, and the powers he granted her gave rise to rumours at court that he had been bewitched. Modern historians caution that assessments of the reign have often credited to Suleiman alone what was the work of many who served him.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/suleymaniye-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleymaniye-mosque.jpg" alt="Süleymaniye Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S%C3%BCleymaniyeMosqueIstanbul_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hunanuk, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The last decades of the reign were marked by the struggle over the succession. Until the reign of Ahmed I the empire had no formal means of naming a successor, and successions usually involved the death of competing princes. Four of his sons lived into the 1550s: Mustafa, the eldest, born to Mahidevran, and Selim, Bayezid and Cihangir, born to Hürrem. Hürrem is usually held at least partly responsible for the intrigues, though there is no evidence to support this. During the Persian campaign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> Rüstem spread rumours that Mustafa was receptive to the idea of replacing his father. Suleiman summoned Mustafa to his tent in the Ereğli valley, where he was killed with a bow-string; the execution is dated 6 October 1553.</p><p>Civil war then broke out between Selim and Bayezid. With the aid of his father's army Selim defeated Bayezid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in 1559, and Bayezid fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> with his four sons. In return for large amounts of gold the Shah allowed a Turkish executioner to strangle Bayezid and his sons in 1561. In 1565 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> from 18 May to 8 September; a relief force from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> turned the battle, with the loss of 10,000 Ottoman troops.</p><p>On 1 May 1566, after an absence of some thirteen years, Suleiman set out on his thirteenth expedition, against Szigetvár in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He died in his tent on 6 September, one day before the fortress fell. The Grand Vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokollu-mehmed-pasha">Sokollu Mehmed Pasha</a> kept the death secret for 48 days, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a> could be enthroned. The body was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and buried in a mausoleum at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Suleiman's conquests brought major Muslim cities, many Balkan provinces and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> under Ottoman control, and gave the empire a powerful presence in the European balance of power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s ambassador Busbecq warned that once the Turks had settled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, "they will fly at our throats supported by the might of the whole East". Hürrem's influence opened the period known as the Sultanate of Women.</p><p>His image as an ideal ruler was shaped in his lifetime by commissioned works, above all those of Celalzade Mustafa, chancellor from 1534 to 1557. Later Ottoman writers of advice literature held up that image, urged sultans to keep the empire's institutions in their sixteenth-century form, and portrayed deviation from it as decline. Western historians long took such claims at face value and held that the empire declined after his death. Since the 1980s that view has been re-examined, and modern scholars overwhelmingly reject it; the following decades are described as an era of crisis, adaptation and transformation.</p><p>His names have their own history. "The Magnificent" is the Western epithet; Kanuni, "the Lawgiver", is absent from Ottoman sources of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries and may date from the early eighteenth. The mausoleum raised at Turbék, near Szigetvár, where his heart and other organs were buried, became a place of pilgrimage. His is one of the 23 relief portraits of figures noted for their part in the principles of American law over the gallery doors of the House Chamber of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-capitol">United States Capitol</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Suleiman I ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from 1520 to 1566, the longest reign of any sultan. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, failed before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, codified sultanic law and built the Süleymaniye. He had two of his sons put to death, and historians no longer hold that the empire declined after him.</em></p>

